    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Islamism is not one movement: it spans reformists who work through elections, Salafis who differ on whether to engage in politics at all, and jihadists who reject democracy, and moderates now make up the majority.</li><li>Its early thinkers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a> and Abul A’la Maududi, answered the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> with the ideal of an Islamic state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a> added armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> of 1978–1979 created the first modern Islamist state, and after it, in one account, “nobody within the Muslim world or outside it” remained unaware of militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>“Islamism” first appeared in English, as “Islamismus”, in 1696 and long meant simply the religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; it had all but vanished from English by 1938 and returned, in its political sense, only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> of 1978–79.</p></aside>
    </section>

