    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> begins his treatise “The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> or the Supreme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>” in Al-Manar, later a blueprint for Islamist movements</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">The Turkish National Assembly abolishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>, shocking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> clerical world</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1928</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a> founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> in Ismailiyah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">Abul A’la Maududi founds the Jamaat-e-Islami party</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a> is executed for allegedly taking part in a plot to assassinate the president</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">A revolution led by Ayatollah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> overthrows the Shah and creates the Islamic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">An armed fundamentalist group seizes the Grand Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and holds it for over a week</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2013</span><span class="rf-text">A coup deposes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>’s first democratically elected president</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani.jpg" alt="Jamal al-Din al-Afghani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_afghani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islamism had forerunners: the jurist Ibn Taymiyyah in the 13th and 14th centuries, Ahmad Sirhindi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahhabism">Wahhabism</a>, Shah Waliullah, and the Deobandi movement founded around 1867. At the end of the 19th century, as European colonial powers dismembered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a> (1837–97), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> (1849–1905) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> (1865–1935) preached Islamic alternatives to that decline.</p><p>The abolition of the Ottoman Sultanate in 1922 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> by the Turkish National Assembly in March 1924 shocked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> clerical world, and many felt the need to present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> not as a traditional religion but as a modern socio-political ideology. In 1922–1923 Rida published in Al-Manar a treatise on “The Caliphate or the Supreme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>”, advocating a restored caliphate guided by Islamic jurists; the doctrine became the blueprint of later Islamist movements. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khilafat-movement">Khilafat movement</a> of 1919–24 expressed Muslims’ hopes for the caliphate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a>, who in 1930 outlined a Muslim state in north-western India, influenced Maududi.</p><p>Abul A’la Maududi’s al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a> fil-Islam of 1925 was his first contribution to Islamism, and he founded the Jamaat-e-Islami in 1941. In 1928 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> in Ismailiyah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, arguably the first, largest and most influential modern Islamic political and religious organisation. Olivier Roy dates the beginning of the Islamist movement “more or less in 1940”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islamists hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is innately political and, as a political system, superior to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, liberal democracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. In its original formulation Islamism sought to revive Islam to its past assertiveness and glory, purify it of foreign elements and reorder “government and society in accordance with laws prescribed by Islam”, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. Rida described a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> governing through consultation, shura, and applying sharia with jurists who would update law by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>; Maududi held that a society could not be Islamic without sharia and an Islamic state to enforce it, founded on tawhid, the unity of God, prophethood and the caliphate. Under the motto “the Qur’an is our constitution” the Brotherhood sought revival through preaching and community services. For Islamists the primary threat of the West is cultural rather than political or economic.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a> argued that since sharia was not in force, Islam did not truly exist in the Muslim world, which lay in jahiliyya, pre-Islamic ignorance, and that preaching must be joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> to destroy its structures. Khomeini’s doctrine of Velayat-e Faqih gave supreme authority to the leading Islamic jurist. Islamists themselves prefer “Islamic movement” or “Islamic activism” to the word Islamism, holding their cause to be Islam renewed, and al-Banna and Maududi favoured a gradual, reformist strategy where al-Turabi favoured state power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Brotherhood spread across the Arab world. Israel’s defeat of Arab armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 was blamed by some on the secular nationalism of their regimes, and Ba’athism, Arab socialism and Arab nationalism lost credibility while Islamist movements inspired by Maududi and Qutb gained ground. From the mid-1970s Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>’s oil wealth funded the spread of a strict Wahhabi or Salafi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, by some estimates over US$100 billion between the 1960s and 2016. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> encouraged Islamic fundamentalists as a hedge against Soviet expansion and a counterweight to nationalist and socialist movements.</p><p>Islamism divided into currents. Salafis agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> must be applied thoroughly but split into quietists, who ignore government, activists, who take part in politics, and Salafi jihadists, inspired by Qutb. Salafi jihadism rose in the 1980s, when an estimated 16,000 to 35,000 Muslim volunteers came to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and after the Soviet withdrawal of 1989 many carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> elsewhere, against the United States and the governments of Muslim-majority countries, which they saw as apostate. Shi’i Islamism, associated above all with Khomeini, drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamists but was “more leftist and more clerical”; the majority Usuli Shi’ism rejects an Islamist state during the Occultation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Hidden Imam</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi.jpg" alt="Mohamed Morsi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohamed_Morsi-05-2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilson Dias/ABr, CC BY 3.0 br</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first modern Islamist state, with the possible exception of Zia’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, which brought in the Hudud Ordinances in 1979, was the Islamic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, founded in the revolution of 1978–1979 under a constitution that placed a Supreme Leader, the guardian jurist, over the government. Consolidating power, it executed more than 3,400 political dissidents between June 1981 and March 1982; it later brought almost universal access to electricity and clean water, and it created or assisted like-minded armed Shia groups, SCIRI in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. The Sudanese National Congress implemented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Islamist parties won 75% of the seats in 2011–12, and the Brotherhood’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> became the first democratically elected president before being deposed in 2013. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>’s Justice and Development Party offered a “Turkish model” that came “unstuck” after a purge and violations of democratic principles.</p><p>Militant groups used violence. Elements of the Brotherhood assassinated Egypt’s premier Mahmoud Fahmy El Nokrashy in 1948; Egyptian Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a> assassinated Anwar al-Sadat in 1981; Islamists fought the Algerian Civil War of 1991–2002 and waged a terror campaign in Egypt in the 1990s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> formally declared the “far enemy” under attack in 1996, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osama-bin-laden">Osama bin Laden</a>’s terror attacks climaxed in the September 11 attacks of 2001. After the Arab Spring and the Syrian civil war its Iraqi remnants grew into the Islamic State of Iraq and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, which most Islamists reject as blasphemous.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="Quran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birmingham_Quran_manuscript_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islamism has been criticised for repression of free expression and individual rights, rigidity, hypocrisy, antisemitism, misinterpretation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Sunnah, and innovations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> that Islamists themselves claim to oppose. Others see it as a modern deviation from Islam: Daniel Pipes calls it a modern ideology owing more to European utopian “isms” than to traditional Islam, while Salman Sayyid argues that it is no secular substitute for Islam but “a constellation of political projects that seek to position Islam in the centre of any social order”. Even the term is disputed. The Council on American–Islamic Relations complained that it had become shorthand for “Muslims we don’t like”, and critics say it may feed Islamophobia and stereotyping.</p><p>Debate continues over Islamism and democracy. On the “inclusion-moderation” theory, the more Islamists are included in politics, the more moderate they become; critics, both Islamists who reject democracy and anti-Islamists, hold its aims incompatible with democratic principles. Jihadist groups such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>, Egyptian Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a> and the Taliban reject democracy as unbelief. Hayri Abaza argues that a failure to tell Islam from Islamism leads many in the West to support illiberal Islamist regimes out of respect for Islam, to the cost of those who would separate religion from politics. Morten Valbjørn calls the division into radical and moderate, or violent and peaceful, Islamism “simplistic”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Robin Wright has argued that Islamist movements “arguably altered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> more than any trend since the modern states gained independence”. By the early 21st century “secular”, once a label proudly worn, was shunned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and much of the Muslim world; in 2009 the journalist Sonja Zekri wrote that Islamists “determine how one dresses, what one eats” even where they never take power. Scholars explain its appeal by the charitable work of movements such as the Brotherhood, which run clinics, schools and legal aid, by economic stagnation, and by rural migrants uprooted into vast cities; Gilles Kepel notes that the Islamist uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> came with the first generation taught en masse to read and write.</p><p>By 2020 observers such as Olivier Roy, Mustafa Akyol and Nader Hashemi saw a decline in its vigour. Roy argues that in four decades it failed to build a “concrete and viable blueprint for society”; Hashemi writes that “the popular prestige of political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> has been tarnished by its experience with state power”. Arab Barometer polls in six Arab countries found that by 2018–19 fewer than 20% trusted Islamist parties, and that the share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> calling themselves not religious had risen from 8% in 2013 to 13%. Several parties, Ennahda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and the Prosperous Justice Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> among them, formally gave up their aim of implementing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>, a turn called post-Islamism.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Islamism is a range of movements holding that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> should shape politics and the state through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. Formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna">Hassan al-Banna</a>, Maududi and Qutb after the end of the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, it took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1979 and entered elections across the Muslim world, while militant Salafi jihadists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> to ISIL waged armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>; since about 2020 observers have seen it decline.</em></p>

